  "notes": [
   "Step 1 listed 10 jumper wires, including wire9 and wire10 as spares. I dropped them here because neither the circuit image nor the text gives them any connection. The 8 wires kept are the ones the circuit needs. The parts list of 10 is a pack size.",
   "The image shows no breadboards. I assumed the two mini breadboards from the parts list: bb1 on the reader side holds both 4.7 k\u03a9 pull-ups and the 3V3 rail, and bb2 on the writer side is a junction. wire7 and wire8 carry SCL and SDA between them. Exact hole positions are my choice.",
   "SCL is on A5 and SDA is on A4 of both boards, as the tutorial says. Each line has one 4.7 k\u03a9 resistor pulling it up to 3V3 (the rail on bb1, fed by the reader's 3V3 pin). The image shows the same layout.",
   "GND is a single direct wire between the two boards' GND pins (the image shows it that way). 3.3 V pull-ups suit the 3.3 V-only Nano 33 IoT.",
   "The resistors are placed with their legs in the same bb1 column strips as the SCL and SDA wires, and the other leg in the top positive rail. In the catalogue every 'tp' hole is one strip, so tp.1, tp.2 and tp.3 are the same node.",
   "Each board is powered by its own USB cable from the computer. The computer is not a graph node, so each USB cable connects only to its board's USB port."
  ]
 },
